        // -- ArrayDeque is generally faster than LinkedList for Deque operations
        // -- Although both LinkedList and ArrayDeque implement the Deque interface
        // -- in Java (allowing insertion, deletion, and access at both ends),
        // -- they rely on fundamentally different underlying data structures,
        // -- leading to significant performance and memory differences.

        // -- Use ArrayDeque by default whenever you need a Queue, Deque, or Stack in Java.
        // -- Java's official documentation recommends ArrayDeque over both LinkedList and the legacy Stack class.
        // -- Use LinkedList only if you strictly need to store null values
        // -- or need guaranteed O(1) insertions/removals in the middle of a list using a ListIterator.


        // -- Queue is an interface; LinkedList and ArrayDeque are common implementations
        // -- When you instantiate an object with
//         Queue<String> queue = new ArrayDeque<>(),
        // -- the type of the reference variable (Queue) determines which methods you can access at compile time,
        // -- not the underlying object type (ArrayDeque).

        // -- Because the Queue interface only defines standard FIFO queue operations (like add(), offer(), poll(), and peek()),
        // -- methods specific to double-ended queues—such as addFirst() and addLast() — are not exposed through that reference.


        Deque<String> ll2 = new LinkedList<>(ll);
        ll2.addFirst("Italy");
        ll2.addLast("Germany");
